                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_structural:    `<strong>That's the diagnosis the data supports.</strong> The CEO named leadership skills, but exit themes and decision-latency figures show leaders who aren't allowed to decide. Some of this is capability — most of it is structure. Now you can design for what is actually broken.`,
                    coach_r1_skills:        `<strong>You've accepted my framing — and that is exactly the trap.</strong> You'll design a fine leadership-skills programme. But your own data shows these leaders have no delegation mandate and every safety call gets escalated for fear of blame. Train decisiveness for six months; the moment they return to a structure that punishes it, nothing changes. Did you diagnose the gap, or accept the symptom?`,
                    coach_r1_motivation:    `<strong>“They aren't trying” is the comfortable answer.</strong> When 180 leaders all escalate, that is a system signal, not a motivation failure. The data points to decision rights and a blame culture — diagnose the structure, not the people.`,
                    coach_r1_selection:     `<strong>Blaming selection lets everyone off the hook.</strong> You can't re-promote 180 people, and the data doesn't support it anyway — it points to the structure they were promoted into. Diagnose decision rights and culture, not the hiring.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp, honest diagnosis.</strong> You named the structural reality AND put a realistic boundary on what learning can fix alone. That candour about the structural remainder is exactly what wins a sceptical board's trust — and what stops you staking the fee on something a classroom can't move.`,
                    coach_r1_overclaim:     `<strong>Careful — you're claiming a programme can fix almost all of it.</strong> That over-promises on the structural part no curriculum can touch. Name the slice that needs decision-rights and culture change from Brackenmoor itself, or you'll own a gap you can't close.`,
                    coach_r1_underclaim:    `<strong>You've written off most of the gap as unfixable.</strong> If a programme can barely move the needle, why are we paying £1.8M for one? There is a real capability slice here — be honest about its size, up and down.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcandour:    `<strong>You're going to tell me only what I want to hear.</strong> Low candour means you'll sell me a skills programme and stay quiet about the structure. That's how you lose the fee and the account when nothing moves. Name what training cannot fix.`,
                    coach_r2_blend:         `<strong>A blended design for all 180 within the cap is the disciplined answer.</strong> You kept reach without buying premium depth you can't afford — now everything rides on whether the blend actually drives transfer.`,
                    coach_r2_fewdeep:       `<strong>Depth for 110, and you've had to tell me 70 won't be developed.</strong> That's an honest trade and sometimes the right one — but be ready to defend which 70 mid-level leaders Brackenmoor leaves behind.`,
                    coach_r2_alldeep:       `<strong>Full flagship depth for all 180 is superb — at flagship prices.</strong> That design wants far more than £10k a head. Either it won't fit £1.8M, or something has to give. Which is it?`,
                    coach_r2_light:         `<strong>A cheap digital-only programme is within budget and within reach — and almost worthless here.</strong> Self-paced content with no manager reinforcement won't change how a burned-out operations leader behaves on site. You've bought reach and thrown away transfer.`,
                    coach_r2_transferled:   `<strong>Transfer-led design within the cap — this is the discipline.</strong> Manager reinforcement, application projects on real problems and accountability are where learning becomes behaviour on a Brackenmoor site. Workshops support it; they don't replace it.`,
                    coach_r2_workshop:      `<strong>You've loaded the design with contact hours.</strong> Ten excellent workshop days evaporate within weeks without application, manager reinforcement and accountability. Where does the learning actually become changed behaviour back on the job?`,
                    coach_r2_digital:       `<strong>A digital-heavy design scales cheaply but transfers poorly.</strong> Self-paced modules inform; they rarely change behaviour without a manager and an accountability loop around them. Rebalance toward on-the-job transfer.`,
                    coach_r2_overbudget:    `<strong>Your design is superb — at well over £10k a head.</strong> Brackenmoor has £1.8M, full stop. As costed, this cannot be contracted: you deliver it to fewer leaders, or you redesign. Which depth do you trade for reach?`,
                    coach_r2_underspend:    `<strong>You've come in far under budget.</strong> Spending a fraction of £1.8M on 180 leaders looks like a thin programme — and a thin programme won't drive the transfer the fee depends on. Use the budget the client has committed.`,
                    coach_r3_behres:        `<strong>You're measuring behaviour and results, not just smiles.</strong> Most programmes stop at reaction and learning. Reaching levels 3–4 is the only way to prove the change the board — and the at-risk fee — actually cares about.`,
                    coach_r3_learning:      `<strong>A satisfaction score and a knowledge test will both look great.</strong> But the £360,000 is tied to business impact, and the board wants decision latency and safety to move. You've committed to prove something you aren't measuring. What level-3 and level-4 metric are you staking the fee on?`,
                    coach_r3_reaction:      `<strong>A smile-sheet measures whether they enjoyed it.</strong> It tells you nothing about whether anything changed on a Brackenmoor site. You cannot stake £360,000 on “did they like it?” Reach behaviour and results.`,
                    coach_r3_metric_sharp:  `<strong>A leading behavioural indicator is the right thing to stake.</strong> Decision latency and delegation are things your leaders actually control — measurable early, and attributable to the programme rather than to procurement or the supply chain.`,
                    coach_r3_overruns:      `<strong>You've tied £360,000 to a 30% drop in project overruns in nine months.</strong> Overruns are driven by procurement, scheduling and supply chain as much as leadership. If the metric doesn't move, you lose the fee even if the leaders genuinely improved. Is that the metric to stake — or do you need a leading indicator you can attribute?`,
                    coach_r3_safety:        `<strong>Staking the fee on the group safety-incident rate is brave but loose.</strong> Safety is shaped by engineering controls, staffing and process as much as leadership behaviour — and nine months is short. You may lose £360,000 to confounders even if you changed behaviour. Choose something you can attribute.`,
                    coach_r3_satisfaction:  `<strong>A 90% satisfaction score is the easiest number to hit and the emptiest to stake.</strong> It proves people enjoyed the room, not that anything changed on site. The board will see straight through committing the fee to a smile-sheet.`,
                    coach_r3_correlation:   `<strong>Pointing at a correlation isn't attribution.</strong> When the number moves I'll ask how you know it was your programme and not the new operations director I also hired. Without isolation — a comparison group or a defensible pre-post — your claim is a hopeful coincidence.`,
                    coach_r3_nobaseline:    `<strong>No baseline, no claim.</strong> If you don't capture decision latency and the other metrics at month 0, you have nothing to measure movement against at month 6 — and the at-risk fee becomes unwinnable by design.`,
                    coach_r4_attributed:    `<strong>A defensible ROI range tied to your attribution method.</strong> That is a number I can take to the board and stand behind — honest about uncertainty, grounded in how you isolated the effect, not a single figure plucked for effect.`,
                    coach_r4_precise:       `<strong>You've told me this returns 4.2x.</strong> How do you know it was your programme and not the operations director I also hired? With no attribution method, that precise figure is theatre — and the moment the board's analyst probes it, your credibility goes with it.`,
                    coach_r4_noroi:         `<strong>“The experience will speak for itself” won't survive this room.</strong> I'm staking £360,000 and 22% of your turnover rides on this account. No ROI at all reads as no confidence. Put a defensible, attributed number on the table.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>That's a proposal I can trust.</strong> You named the structural change we have to make ourselves, staked the fee on a metric you can attribute, and put a defensible ROI behind it. That candour is exactly why I'd pick you over a rival waving a guaranteed number.`,
                    coach_r4_overpromise:   `<strong>You've promised to move every number I care about.</strong> Overruns, safety, latency, all of it — from a classroom, in nine months, alone. The moment one of them doesn't move, you've lost the fee and my trust. I needed candour about what you can't fix, not a clean sweep.`,
                    coach_r4_duck:          `<strong>You dodged the structural question.</strong> Keeping the focus on how good the curriculum is doesn't answer whether it can move my operational numbers given the structure these leaders return to. Ducking it now means a harder conversation at month six.`,
                    coach_r4_match:         `<strong>You matched the rivals' guarantee.</strong> Promising whatever ROI it takes to win is exactly the theatre I'm sceptical of — and you've now staked £360,000 on a number you can't defend. A guarantee you can't attribute is a liability dressed as a differentiator.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>You've undercut on price to win.</strong> Cutting the design until you're the cheapest bid strips out the very transfer mechanisms the impact depends on. You may win the mandate and then fail to deliver — and lose the renewal of the whole account.`,

